Description: Fix assert macro undefined errors in abseil and perfetto headers
 When is_official_build=true, Chromium's base/logging.h and cef_logging.h
 undef the assert macro and replace it with DLOG_ASSERT. This breaks
 abseil's str_format and perfetto's tracing code which use assert() in
 constexpr contexts.
 .
 Previous attempts using #include <assert.h> after all other includes
 did not work reliably - the build still failed with "use of undeclared
 identifier 'assert'" errors even though the include was present.
 .
 Fix by explicitly defining assert as ((void)0) when it's not defined.
 This provides a constexpr-safe no-op that works in all contexts.
Author: Juan Manuel Mendez Rey <vejeta@gmail.com>
Bug-Debian: https://bugs.debian.org/915400
Forwarded: not-needed
Last-Update: 2025-12-07

diff --git a/third_party/abseil-cpp/absl/strings/internal/str_format/bind.h b/third_party/abseil-cpp/absl/strings/internal/str_format/bind.h
index a1fcfbc..a246998 100644
--- a/third_party/abseil-cpp/absl/strings/internal/str_format/bind.h
+++ b/third_party/abseil-cpp/absl/strings/internal/str_format/bind.h
@@ -31,11 +31,12 @@
 #include "absl/types/span.h"
 #include "absl/utility/utility.h"

-// Debian: Re-include <assert.h> after all other includes to ensure the assert
-// macro is defined. Chromium headers may have undefined it earlier.
-// NOTE: Must use <assert.h> not <cassert> because <cassert> has header guards
-// that prevent re-inclusion, while <assert.h> is designed to be re-includeable.
-#include <assert.h>
+// Debian: Explicitly define assert as a no-op for official builds.
+// Chromium's logging headers undef assert, and #include <assert.h> doesn't work
+// reliably in all contexts. This explicit define ensures assert is always available.
+#ifndef assert
+#define assert(x) ((void)0)
+#endif

